# Reference verification, batch 1: s001-s006 (Nov 2026)
upd("n004", append="[Checked: no primary document on Gorris’s order was found online (incipit, colophon and dedication of A/B give only ‘Aragonensis… artium et sacre theologie professor’). Still open.]")
upd("n041", append="[Checked: Liber Extra (Friedberg text via SCTA) X 5.33.23 *Antiqua* = Lateran IV c. 5: ‘Romanam ecclesiam, quae … utpote mater universorum Christi fidelium et magistra’. Gorris’s exact wording *cunctorum/omnium fidelium mater est et magistra* is closer to Lateran IV c. 2 (X 1.1.2 *Damnamus*, ‘quam Romana tenet ecclesia, quae disponente Domino cunctorum fidelium mater est et magistra’), also in X 1.3.5 and 3.31.18. Standard curial formula.]", status="verified")
upd("n047", append="[Checked: not found in Scotus, *Ord.* I (Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA), nor in Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* (Quaracchi via SCTA); a different rhymed division is in the commentaries’ *Divisio quattuor librorum*. Source still unidentified.]")
upd("n070", append="[Checked: no further evidence sought beyond standard reference works; the Sorbonne connection is not documented in Henry’s Summa or Quodlibeta. Remains open.]")
upd("n075", append="[Checked: Henry’s argument is *Summa* a. 3 q. 4 (‘Utrum contingat hominem scire omnia ex puris naturalibus’), ed. Badius (Paris 1520) via SCTA: ‘ab agente per instrumentum non potest procedere actio ad quam instrumentum se extendere non potest … lumine naturali … limitatum est’. Ware, *In I Sent.* prol. q. 2, is unedited and could not be checked; status stays open for that half.]")
upd("n077", text="Gorris’s ‘prima parte q. 54 a. 2’ must mean the *Prima Secundae*: Thomas, *ST* I-II q. 54 a. 2 (‘Utrum habitus distinguantur secundum obiecta’), co.: habit ‘et est forma quaedam, et est habitus’, so habits are distinguished like forms ‘secundum diversa principia activa’; ad 2: the astronomer and the natural philosopher prove the earth round by different *media*, ‘sicut diversa principia activa’ (Leonine text, Corpus Thomisticum). This is exactly the exposition in *Ord.* prol. p. 1 q. un. (Wadding/Vivès text via SCTA), ‘Dicta autem responsio ad secundum exponitur sic: habitus et est habitus, et est forma …’, and Gorris’s ‘soluendo argumentum 2um’ fits. The first reply (‘quere responsum Thome’) is *ST* I q. 1 a. 1 ad 2 (‘diversa ratio cognoscibilis diversitatem scientiarum inducit … terra est rotunda’), likewise verified. *ST* I q. 54 a. 2 (on angels) is not meant. The English keeps Gorris’s ‘First Part’.", status="verified")
upd("n078", append="[Checked: Godfrey, *Quodl.* XIV in Hoffmans’s edition (Philosophes Belges V, 1932–35, archive.org) has only 5 qq.; q. 4 is ‘Utrum praeter iustitiam sit alia virtus appetitiva generalis’ (p. 357), unrelated. Searches of Quodl. XI–XIV for the habitus/forma/*principia activa* argument and the ‘terra rotunda’ example found nothing. Godfrey VIII–X not searched in full. Still open; ‘c. 9’ may reflect a different division.]")
upd("n095", text="Richard of St Victor, *De Trinitate* I c. 2, which has both *a summae sanctitatis viris* and *Domine, si error est, a te decepti sumus* (n105); ‘c. 3°’ in A is a slip, and Gorris’s own later ‘primo de trini. c. 2’ is right. Checked through Bonaventure, *Qq. de myst. Trin.* q. 1 a. 2 (Quaracchi V), which quotes both sentences as ‘Richardus primo libro de Trinitate capitulo secundo’; Ribaillier’s edition (1958) was not consulted directly. Scotus cites the same chapter at *Ord.* prol. nn. 105, 113.", status="verified")
upd("n105", append="[Checked: Aug., *De utilitate credendi* 17.35 (PL 42, text at augustinus.it): ‘dubitabimus nos eius Ecclesiae condere gremio …’. Verified; ‘8°’ still unexplained.]", status="verified")
upd("n134", append="[Checked: Giles’s *Quodlibeta* (Louvain 1646) are not available online in searchable form (SCTA holds only Quodl. I q. 6). Still open.]")
upd("n136", text="Henry of Ghent, *Summa* a. 19 qq. 1–2 (‘De quo est theologia ut de subiecto’; ‘… ut de materia’), ed. Badius 1520 via SCTA: God is the subject ‘sub ea ratione … sub qua omnia cognoscendo in ipsa ad ipsum potest fieri attributio’, i.e. under a ratio to which everything is related by *attributio*. These are Gorris’s ‘duabus primis questionibus’. Henry there also rejects the view that God is subject ‘solum inquantum redemptor aut reparator’, the Giles view of n134. *Ord.* prol. n. 159 ff.", status="verified")
upd("n137", append="[Checked: Bonaventure, *In I Sent.* prooem. q. 1 (Quaracchi I, via SCTA), conclusion: ‘Deus est subiectum theologiae radicale, Christus est subiectum integrale, res et signa sunt subiectum universale sive etiam credibile’. Gorris’s attribution is partly right, as stated.]", status="verified")
upd("n138", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* III q. 6 in Badius 1518 (via SCTA) is on the vegetative and sensitive soul in Christ, which does not fit; no question of Quodl. III treats theology’s object. The reference is wrong as printed and the right locus was not found.]", status="uncertain")
upd("n156", text="Henry of Ghent, *Summa* a. 13 qq. 6–7 (‘Utrum eam addiscens acquirat aliquam notitiam eius super notitiam fidei’; ‘Utrum cum illa ulteriori notitia stet notitia fidei’), ed. Badius 1520 via SCTA: the theologian’s understanding of the *credibilia* grows like light ‘ab ortu diei usque ad meridiem’ (q. 7), which is the image Gorris describes (morning light, noonday light, and a ‘middle’ light advancing between them). Scotus rejects it. Gorris says the rejection is ‘in the *reportationes*’ (*reportatoriis*, confirmed by B), i.e. Scotus’s *Reportatio* (*Report. Paris.* prol.), not the *Ordinatio*: a sign that he had a *Reportatio* to hand. (The Reportatio passage itself was not checked.)", status="verified")
